    {
      "title": "60-day deadline for Iran peace deal is expiring. Here's where things stand",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/60-day-deadline-for-iran-peace-deal-is-expiring-here-s-where-things-stand-126081700633_1.html",
      "description": "The deal that US President Donald Trump signed at Versailles in June set an ambitious 60-day deadline for ending the war with Iran and reaching an accord on its nuclear program.\nThe deadline was Monday, and the two sides are further apart than they were then.\nTalks, such as they are, have focused on reopening the Strait of Hormuz and lifting a US blockade on Iran, both of which were supposed to have happened under the interim deal.\nThere has been no sign of any compromise on the strait, or that detailed nuclear talks have even begun.\nThe US, meanwhile, has no good options for getting out of the war it started alongside Israel.\nAcceding to Iran's latest demands would mean giving it control over a critical international waterway that carried a fifth of the world's traded oil and gas before the war - and would be tantamount to admitting defeat.\nEscalating the deeply unpopular war would further draw down US supplies of advanced missile interceptors, jolt the world economy, and drive",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-08-17T09:14:28"
    }
